// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET
//
// Registry #930, the DECLARED-TYPE receiver — named by the row's acceptance clause
// alongside `f64` and `str`, and it is not a formality: `Meters(2.5).to_i16()`
// was measured on 2026-09-05 to BUILD AND PRINT `true`, exactly the silent wrong
// answer the primitive case gives. A newtype over `f64` reaching an `Ints` blanket is
// the same defect wearing a user's type name.
//
// This fixture also records a wrong turn, because the wrong turn is the instructive
// part. The fix was first narrowed to primitives on the belief that the
// free-function door "stops at declared types" — read off `only_ints(Meters(2.5))`,
// which compiles clean there. It does not stop at declared types. Write the same
// value through a binding and that door refuses it:
//
//     ro m Meters = Meters(2.5)
//     only_ints(m)              // [E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET]
//
// What the free-function door actually loses is a CALL EXPRESSION written directly as
// the argument, and the receiver position loses it identically. That is a separate
// defect on BOTH doors, filed as its own row — and the near miss is worth naming: a
// parity claim measured on one spelling of the value would have shipped a fix that
// under-guarded the very receiver the acceptance names.
//
// The binding is annotated (`ro m Meters = ...`) for exactly that reason: the
// unannotated spelling routes into the other defect, and a fixture standing on a
// second defect is green for the wrong reason the day that one is fixed.
//
// Spec: D310 (`spec/decisions/02-types.md`) — membership is by explicit member list;
// `~underlying`/repr-based membership is deferred to Plan 102, so a newtype does NOT
// inherit its carrier's set.
module neg.p930_blanket_set_bound_newtype_neg

type Meters f64

fn ask_a_newtype_over_a_float_for_an_integer_conversion() Io -> () {
    ro m Meters = Meters(2.5)
    // nova:expect E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET -- `Meters` is not a member of `Ints`; before the fix this built and printed `true`
    println(m.to_i16().is_ok())
}
